<section xml:id="sec-diagonalization">
  <title>Diagonalization</title>

  <objectives>
    <ol>
      <li><p>Learn two main criteria for a matrix to be diagonalizable.</p></li>
      <li><p>Develop a library of examples of matrices that are and are not diagonalizable.</p></li>
      <li><p component="default">Understand what diagonalizability and multiplicity have to say about similarity.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Recipes:</em> diagonalize a matrix, quickly compute powers of a matrix by diagonalization.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Pictures:</em> the geometry of diagonal matrices, why a shear is not diagonalizable.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Theorem:</em> the diagonalization theorem (two variants).</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Vocabulary words:</em> <term>diagonalizable</term>, <term>algebraic multiplicity</term>, <term>geometric multiplicity</term>.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p component="default">
      Diagonal matrices are the easiest kind of matrices to understand: they just scale the coordinate directions by their diagonal entries.
      
        In <xref ref="sec-similarity" text="type-global"/>, we saw that similar matrices behave in the same way, with respect to different coordinate systems.  Therefore, if a matrix is similar to a diagonal matrix, it is also relatively easy to understand.  This section is devoted to the question: <q>When is a matrix similar to a diagonal matrix?</q>
      
      
    </p>
<p component="1553">
      Diagonal matrices are the easiest kind of matrices to understand: they just scale the coordinate directions by their diagonal entries.
      
      
        This section is devoted to the question: <q>When is a matrix <em>similar</em> to a diagonal matrix?</q>  We will see that the algebra and geometry of such a matrix is relatively easy to understand.
      
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-diagonalizability">
    <title>Diagonalizability</title>

      <p component="1553">
        First we make precise what we mean when we say two matrices are <q>similar</q>.
      </p>

      <definition component="1553">
        <idx><h>Similarity</h><h>definition of</h></idx>
        <idx><h>Matrix</h><h>similar</h><see>Similarity</see></idx>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Two <m>n\times n</m> matrices <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are <term>similar</term> if there exists an invertible <m>n\times n</m> matrix <m>C</m> such that <m>A = CBC\inv</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <example component="1553" xml:id="similarity-eg1">
        <p>
          The matrices
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
-12 \amp 15 \\
-10 \amp 13
\end{pmatrix} \sptxt{and} \begin{pmatrix}
3 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp -2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
          are similar because
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
-12 \amp 15 \\
-10 \amp 13
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
-2 \amp 3 \\
1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
3 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp -2
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
-2 \amp 3 \\
1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix}\inv</mrow>
</md>,
          as the reader can verify.
        </p>
      </example>

      <example component="1553">
        <p>
          The matrices
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
3 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp -2
\end{pmatrix} \sptxt{and} \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
          are not similar.  Indeed, the second matrix is the identity matrix <m>I_2</m>, so if <m>C</m> is any invertible <m>2\times 2</m> matrix, then
          <md>
<mrow>CI_2C\inv = CC\inv = I_2 \neq \begin{pmatrix}
3 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp -2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
      </example>

      <p component="1553">
        If two matrices are similar, then their powers are similar as well.
      </p>

      <fact component="1553" xml:id="similarity-powers">
        <idx><h>Similarity</h><h>and powers</h></idx>
        <idx><h>Matrix multiplication</h><h>powers</h><h>and similarity</h></idx>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>A = CBC\inv</m>.  Then for any <m>n\geq 1</m>, we have
            <md>
<mrow>A^n = CB^n C\inv</mrow>
</md>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>
            First note that
            <md>
<mrow>A^2 = AA = (CBC\inv)(CBC\inv) = CB(C\inv C)BC\inv = CBI_nBC\inv = CB^2C\inv</mrow>
</md>.
            Next we have
            <md>
<mrow>A^3 = A^2A = (CB^2C\inv)(CBC\inv) = CB^2(C\inv C)BC\inv = CB^3C\inv</mrow>
</md>.
            The pattern is clear.
          </p>
        </proof>
      </fact>

      <p component="1553">
        In this chapter, we will determine when a matrix is similar to a diagonal matrix.  This property is important enough to deserve its own name.
      </p>


      <p component="default">
        Before answering the above question, first we give it a name.
      </p>

    <definition>
      <idx><h>Diagonalizability</h><h>definition of</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Similarity</h><h>to a diagonal matrix</h><see>Diagonalizability</see></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          An <m>n\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> is <term>diagonalizable</term> if it is similar to a diagonal matrix: that is, if there exists an invertible <m>n\times n</m> matrix <m>C</m> and a diagonal matrix <m>D</m> such that
          <md>
<mrow>A = CDC\inv</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <observation>
      <idx><h>Diagonalizability</h><h>diagonal matrices</h></idx>
      <p component="default">
        Any diagonal matrix is <m>D</m> is diagonalizable because it is
        
          <xref ref="similarity-eq-reln" text="title">similar to itself</xref>.
        
        
        For instance,
        <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 3
\end{pmatrix} = I_3\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 3
\end{pmatrix}I_3\inv</mrow>
</md>.
      </p>
<p component="1553">
        Any diagonal matrix is <m>D</m> is diagonalizable because it is
        
        
          similar to itself.
        
        For instance,
        <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 3
\end{pmatrix} = I_3\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 3
\end{pmatrix}I_3\inv</mrow>
</md>.
      </p>
    </observation>

    <example>
      <p component="default">
        
          Most of the examples in <xref ref="sec-similarity" text="type-global"/> involve diagonalizable matrices:
        
        
        <md>
<mrow>\def\idb{\quad\parbox{\widthof{because it equals}}{is diagonalizable\\because it equals}\quad}
\begin{pmatrix}
-12 \amp 15 \\
-10 \amp 13
\end{pmatrix} \amp\idb
          \begin{pmatrix}
-2 \amp 3 \\
1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
3 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp -2
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
-2 \amp 3 \\
1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix}\inv</mrow>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1/2 \amp 3/2 \\
3/2 \amp 1/2
\end{pmatrix} \amp\idb
          \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix}\inv</mrow>
<mrow>\frac 15\begin{pmatrix}
-8 \amp -9 \\
6 \amp 13
\end{pmatrix} \amp\idb
          \frac 12\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \amp -3 \\
2 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix} \left(\frac 12\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \amp -3 \\
2 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}\right)\inv</mrow>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
-1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
-1 \amp 1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} \amp\idb
          \begin{pmatrix}
-1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
-1 \amp 0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
-1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
-1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
-1 \amp 0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}\inv</mrow>
</md>.
      </p>
<p component="1553">
        
        
          The following are examples of diagonalizable matrices:
        
        <md>
<mrow>\def\idb{\quad\parbox{\widthof{because it equals}}{is diagonalizable\\because it equals}\quad}
\begin{pmatrix}
-12 \amp 15 \\
-10 \amp 13
\end{pmatrix} \amp\idb
          \begin{pmatrix}
-2 \amp 3 \\
1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
3 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp -2
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
-2 \amp 3 \\
1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix}\inv</mrow>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1/2 \amp 3/2 \\
3/2 \amp 1/2
\end{pmatrix} \amp\idb
          \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix}\inv</mrow>
<mrow>\frac 15\begin{pmatrix}
-8 \amp -9 \\
6 \amp 13
\end{pmatrix} \amp\idb
          \frac 12\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \amp -3 \\
2 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix} \left(\frac 12\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \amp -3 \\
2 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}\right)\inv</mrow>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
-1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
-1 \amp 1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} \amp\idb
          \begin{pmatrix}
-1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
-1 \amp 0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
-1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
-1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
-1 \amp 0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}\inv</mrow>
</md>.
      </p>
    </example>

    <observation xml:id="diag-of-similar">
      <idx><h>Diagonalizability</h><h>similar matrices</h></idx>
      <p component="default">
        If a matrix <m>A</m> is diagonalizable, and if <m>B</m> is similar to <m>A</m>, then <m>B</m> is diagonalizable
        
          as well by <xref ref="similarity-eq-reln"/>.
        
        
      </p>
<p component="1553">
        If a matrix <m>A</m> is diagonalizable, and if <m>B</m> is similar to <m>A</m>, then <m>B</m> is diagonalizable
        
        
          as well.  Indeed, if <m>A = CDC\inv</m> for <m>D</m> diagonal, and <m>B = EAE\inv</m>, then
          <md>
<mrow>B = EAE\inv = E(CDC\inv)E\inv = (EC)D(EC)\inv</mrow>
</md>,
          so <m>B</m> is similar to <m>D</m>.
        
      </p>
    </observation>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Powers of diagonalizable matrices</title>
    <p component="default">
      Multiplying diagonal matrices together just multiplies their diagonal entries:
      <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp x_2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp x_3
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
y_1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp y_2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp y_3
\end{pmatrix}
        = \begin{pmatrix}
x_1y_1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp x_2y_2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp x_3y_3
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
      Therefore, it is easy to take powers of a diagonal matrix:
      <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
x \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp y \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp z
\end{pmatrix}^n = \begin{pmatrix}
x^n \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp y^n \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp z^n
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
      By <xref ref="similarity-powers"/>, if <m>A = CDC\inv</m> then <m>A^n = CD^nC\inv</m>, so it is also easy to take powers of <em>diagonalizable</em> matrices.

This will be very important in applications to difference equations in <xref ref="sec-stochastic-matrices" text="type-global"/>.


    </p>
<p component="1553">
      Multiplying diagonal matrices together just multiplies their diagonal entries:
      <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp x_2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp x_3
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
y_1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp y_2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp y_3
\end{pmatrix}
        = \begin{pmatrix}
x_1y_1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp x_2y_2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp x_3y_3
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
      Therefore, it is easy to take powers of a diagonal matrix:
      <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
x \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp y \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp z
\end{pmatrix}^n = \begin{pmatrix}
x^n \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp y^n \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp z^n
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
      By <xref ref="similarity-powers"/>, if <m>A = CDC\inv</m> then <m>A^n = CD^nC\inv</m>, so it is also easy to take powers of <em>diagonalizable</em> matrices.


This is often very important in applications.

    </p>

    <algorithm xml:id="diag-powers">
      <title>Compute powers of a diagonalizable matrix</title>
      <idx><h>Diagonalizability</h><h>powers of</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Matrix multiplication</h><h>powers</h><h>and diagonalizability</h></idx>
      <p>
        If <m>A = CDC\inv</m>, where <m>D</m> is a diagonal matrix, then <m>A^n = CD^nC\inv</m>:
        <md>
<mrow>A = C\begin{pmatrix}
x \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp y \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp z
\end{pmatrix}C\inv \quad\implies\quad
          A^n = C\begin{pmatrix}
x^n \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp y^n \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp z^n
\end{pmatrix}C\inv</mrow>
</md>.
      </p>
    </algorithm>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1/2 \amp 3/2 \\
3/2 \amp 1/2
\end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix}\inv</mrow>
</md>.
          Find a formula for <m>A^n</m> in which the entries are functions of <m>n</m>, where <m>n</m> is any positive whole number.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We have
          <md>
<mrow>A^n \amp= \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix}^n \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix}\inv</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
2^n \amp 0 \\
0 \amp (-1)^n
\end{pmatrix} \frac 1{-2}\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \amp -1 \\
-1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \begin{pmatrix}
2^n \amp (-1)^n \\
2^n \amp (-1)^{n+1}
\end{pmatrix}\frac 12\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \frac 12\begin{pmatrix}
2^n+(-1)^n \amp 2^n+(-1)^{n+1} \\
2^n+(-1)^{n+1} \amp 2^n+(-1)^{n}
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>,
          where we used <m>(-1)^{n+2}=(-1)^2(-1)^n = (-1)^n</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>
      A fundamental question about a matrix is whether or not it is diagonalizable.  The following is the primary criterion for diagonalizability.  It shows that diagonalizability is an eigenvalue problem.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="diagonalization-thm">
      <idx><h>Diagonalizability</h><h>criterion</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Eigenvector</h><h>and diagonalizability</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          An <m>n\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> is diagonalizable if and only if <m>A</m> has <m>n</m> linearly independent eigenvectors.
        </p>
        <p>
          In this case, <m>A = CDC\inv</m> for
          <md>
<mrow>C = \begin{pmatrix}
| \amp | \amp | \\
v_1 \amp v_2 \amp \cdots \amp v_n \\
| \amp | \amp |
\end{pmatrix}
            \qquad
            D = \begin{pmatrix}
\lambda_1 \amp 0 \amp \cdots \amp 0 \\
0 \amp \lambda_2 \amp \cdots \amp 0 \\
\vdots \amp \vdots \amp \ddots \amp \vdots \\
0 \amp 0 \amp \cdots \amp \lambda_n
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>,
          where <m>v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_n</m> are linearly independent eigenvectors, and <m>\lambda_1,\lambda_2,\ldots,\lambda_n</m> are the corresponding eigenvalues, in the same order.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          First suppose that <m>A</m> has <m>n</m> linearly independent eigenvectors <m>v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_n</m>, with eigenvalues <m>\lambda_1,\lambda_2,\ldots,\lambda_n</m>.  Define <m>C</m> as above, so <m>C</m> is invertible by the <xref ref="imt-2"/>.  Let <m>D = C\inv A C</m>, so <m>A = CDC\inv</m>.  <xref ref="linear-trans-pick-columns" text="title">Multiplying by standard coordinate vectors</xref> picks out the columns of <m>C</m>: we have <m>Ce_i = v_i</m>, so <m>e_i = C\inv v_i</m>.  We multiply by the standard coordinate vectors to find the columns of <m>D</m>:
          <md>
<mrow>De_i = C\inv A Ce_i = C\inv Av_i = C\inv\lambda_i v_i = \lambda_iC\inv v_i = \lambda_ie_i</mrow>
</md>.
          Therefore, the columns of <m>D</m> are multiples of the standard coordinate vectors:
          <md>
<mrow>D = \begin{pmatrix}
\lambda_1 \amp 0 \amp \cdots \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp \lambda_2 \amp \cdots \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
\vdots \amp \vdots \amp \ddots \amp \vdots \amp \vdots \\
0 \amp 0 \amp \cdots \amp \lambda_{n-1} \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp \cdots \amp 0 \amp \lambda_n
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now suppose that <m>A = CDC\inv</m>, where <m>C</m> has columns <m>v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_n</m>, and <m>D</m> is diagonal with diagonal entries <m>\lambda_1,\lambda_2,\ldots,\lambda_n</m>.  Since <m>C</m> is invertible, its columns are linearly independent.  We have to show that <m>v_i</m> is an eigenvector of <m>A</m> with eigenvalue <m>\lambda_i</m>.  We know that the standard coordinate vector <m>e_i</m> is an eigenvector of <m>D</m> with eigenvalue <m>\lambda_i</m>, so:
          <md>
<mrow>Av_i = CDC\inv v_i = CDe_i = C\lambda_ie_i = \lambda_iCe_i = \lambda_i v_i</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <p>
      By <xref ref="evecs-linindep"/>, if an <m>n\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> has <m>n</m> <em>distinct</em> eigenvalues <m>\lambda_1,\lambda_2,\ldots,\lambda_n</m>, then a choice of corresponding eigenvectors <m>v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_n</m> is automatically linearly independent.
    </p>

    <insight>
      <idx><h>Eigenvalue</h><h>and diagonalizability</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Diagonalizability</h><h>distinct eigenvalues</h></idx>
      <p>
        An <m>n\times n</m> matrix with <m>n</m> distinct eigenvalues is diagonalizable.
      </p>
    </insight>

    <example xml:id="diag-easy-eg">
      <title>Easy Example</title>
      <idx><h>Diagonalizability</h><h>diagonal matrices</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Apply the <xref ref="diagonalization-thm"/> to the matrix
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 3
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          This diagonal matrix is in particular upper-triangular, so its eigenvalues are the diagonal entries <m>1,2,3</m>.  The standard coordinate vectors are eigenvalues of a diagonal matrix:
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 3
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
0 \\
0
\end{pmatrix} = 1\cdot\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
0 \\
0
\end{pmatrix} \qquad
            \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 3
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix} = 2\cdot\begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 3
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix} = 3\cdot\begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Therefore, the diagonalization theorem says that <m>A=CDC\inv</m>, where the columns of <m>C</m> are the standard coordinate vectors, and the <m>D</m> is the diagonal matrix with entries <m>1,2,3</m>:
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 3
\end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 3
\end{pmatrix}
            \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}\inv</mrow>
</md>.
          This just tells us that <m>A</m> is similar to itself.
        </p>
        <p>
          Actually, the diagonalization theorem is not completely trivial even for diagonal matrices.  If we put our eigenvalues in the order <m>3,2,1</m>, then the corresponding eigenvectors are <m>e_3,e_2,e_1</m>, so we also have that <m>A = C'D'(C')\inv</m>, where <m>C'</m> is the matrix with columns <m>e_3,e_2,e_1</m>, and <m>D'</m> is the diagonal matrix with entries <m>3,2,1</m>:
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 3
\end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
1 \amp 0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
3 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}
            \begin{pmatrix}
0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
1 \amp 0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}\inv</mrow>
</md>.
          In particular, the matrices
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 3
\end{pmatrix}
            \sptxt{and}
            \begin{pmatrix}
3 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
          are similar to each other.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
    </p>

    <note>
      <title>Non-Uniqueness of Diagonalization</title>
      <idx><h>Diagonalizability</h><h>order of eigenvalues</h></idx>
      <p>
        We saw in the above example that changing the order of the eigenvalues and eigenvectors produces a different diagonalization of the same matrix. There are generally many different ways to diagonalize a matrix, corresponding to different orderings of the eigenvalues of that matrix.  The important thing is that the eigenvalues and eigenvectors have to be listed in the same order.
        <md>
<mrow>A \amp= \begin{pmatrix}
| \amp | \amp | \\
v_1 \amp v_2 \amp v_3 \\
| \amp | \amp |
\end{pmatrix}
          \begin{pmatrix}
\lambda_1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp \lambda_2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp \lambda_3
\end{pmatrix}
          \begin{pmatrix}
| \amp | \amp | \\
v_1 \amp v_2 \amp v_3 \\
| \amp | \amp |
\end{pmatrix}\inv</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \begin{pmatrix}
| \amp | \amp | \\
v_3 \amp v_2 \amp v_1 \\
| \amp | \amp |
\end{pmatrix}
          \begin{pmatrix}
\lambda_3 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp \lambda_2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp \lambda_1
\end{pmatrix}
          \begin{pmatrix}
| \amp | \amp | \\
v_3 \amp v_2 \amp v_1 \\
| \amp | \amp |
\end{pmatrix}\inv</mrow>
</md>.
      </p>
      <p>
        There are other ways of finding different diagonalizations of the same matrix.  For instance, you can scale one of the eigenvectors by a constant <m>c</m>:
        <md>
<mrow>A \amp= \begin{pmatrix}
| \amp | \amp | \\
v_1 \amp v_2 \amp v_3 \\
| \amp | \amp |
\end{pmatrix}
          \begin{pmatrix}
\lambda_1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp \lambda_2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp \lambda_3
\end{pmatrix}
          \begin{pmatrix}
| \amp | \amp | \\
v_1 \amp v_2 \amp v_3 \\
| \amp | \amp |
\end{pmatrix}\inv</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \begin{pmatrix}
| \amp | \amp | \\
cv_1 \amp v_2 \amp v_3 \\
| \amp | \amp |
\end{pmatrix}
          \begin{pmatrix}
\lambda_1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp \lambda_2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp \lambda_3
\end{pmatrix}
          \begin{pmatrix}
| \amp | \amp | \\
cv_1 \amp v_2 \amp v_3 \\
| \amp | \amp |
\end{pmatrix}\inv</mrow>
</md>,
        you can find a different basis entirely for an eigenspace of dimension at least <m>2</m>, etc.
      </p>
    </note>

    <example xml:id="diagonal-eg-22">
      <title>A diagonalizable <m>2\times 2</m> matrix</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Diagonalize the matrix
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1/2 \amp 3/2 \\
3/2 \amp 1/2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We need to find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of <m>A</m>.  First we compute the characteristic polynomial:
          <md>
<mrow>f(\lambda) = \lambda^2-\Tr(A)\lambda + \det(A) = \lambda^2-\lambda-2
            = (\lambda+1)(\lambda-2)</mrow>
</md>.
          Therefore, the eigenvalues are <m>-1</m> and <m>2</m>.  We need to compute eigenvectors for each eigenvalue.  We start with <m>\lambda_1 = -1</m>:
          <md>
<mrow>(A + 1I_2)v = 0 \iff
  \begin{pmatrix}
3/2 \amp 3/2 \\
3/2 \amp 3/2
\end{pmatrix}v = 0
  \;\xrightarrow{\text{RREF}}\;
  \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}v = 0</mrow>
</md>.
          The parametric form is <m>x = -y</m>, so <m>v_1 = {-1\choose 1}</m> is an eigenvector with eigenvalue <m>\lambda_1</m>.  Now we find an eigenvector with eigenvalue <m>\lambda_2 = 2</m>:
          <md>
<mrow>(A-2I_2)v = 0 \iff
  \begin{pmatrix}
-3/2 \amp 3/2 \\
3/2 \amp -3/2
\end{pmatrix}v = 0
  \;\xrightarrow{\text{RREF}}\;
  \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp -1 \\
0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}v = 0</mrow>
</md>.
          The parametric form is <m>x = y</m>, so <m>v_2 = {1\choose 1}</m> is an eigenvector with eigenvalue <m>2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The eigenvectors <m>v_1,v_2</m> are linearly independent, so the <xref ref="diagonalization-thm"/> says that
          <md>
<mrow>A = CDC\inv \sptxt{for} C = \begin{pmatrix}
-1 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} \qquad D = \begin{pmatrix}
-1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Alternatively, if we choose <m>2</m> as our first eigenvalue, then
          <md>
<mrow>A = C'D'(C')\inv \sptxt{for} C' = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp -1 \\
1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} \qquad D' = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
        <figure>
          <caption>The green line is the <m>-1</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m>, and the violet line is the <m>2</m>-eigenspace. There are two linearly independent (noncollinear) eigenvectors visible in the picture: choose any nonzero vector on the green line, and any nonzero vector on the violet line.</caption>
          <interactive
              iframe="iframes/eigenspace-if.html?mat=1/2,3/2:3/2,1/2&amp;nomult"
              width="90%"
              aspect="4:3">
            <shortdescription>
              <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </description>
            <instructions>
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </instructions>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="diagonal-eg-22-zero">
      <title>A diagonalizable <m>2\times 2</m> matrix with a zero eigenvector</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Diagonalize the matrix
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
2/3 \amp -4/3 \\
-2/3 \amp 4/3
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We need to find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of <m>A</m>.  First we compute the characteristic polynomial:
          <md>
<mrow>f(\lambda) = \lambda^2-\Tr(A)\lambda + \det(A) = \lambda^2 -2\lambda
            = \lambda(\lambda-2)</mrow>
</md>.
          Therefore, the eigenvalues are <m>0</m> and <m>2</m>.  We need to compute eigenvectors for each eigenvalue.  We start with <m>\lambda_1 = 0</m>:
          <md>
<mrow>(A - 0I_2)v = 0 \iff
  \begin{pmatrix}
2/3 \amp -4/3 \\
-2/3 \amp 4/3
\end{pmatrix}v = 0
  \;\xrightarrow{\text{RREF}}\;
  \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp -2 \\
0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}v = 0</mrow>
</md>.
          The parametric form is <m>x = 2y</m>, so <m>v_1 = {2\choose 1}</m> is an eigenvector with eigenvalue <m>\lambda_1</m>.  Now we find an eigenvector with eigenvalue <m>\lambda_2 = 2</m>:
          <md>
<mrow>(A-2I_2)v = 0 \iff
  \begin{pmatrix}
-4/3 \amp -4/3 \\
-2/3 \amp -2/3
\end{pmatrix}v = 0
  \;\xrightarrow{\text{RREF}}\;
  \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}v = 0</mrow>
</md>.
          The parametric form is <m>x = -y</m>, so <m>v_2 = {1\choose-1}</m> is an eigenvector with eigenvalue <m>2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The eigenvectors <m>v_1,v_2</m> are linearly independent, so the <xref ref="diagonalization-thm"/> says that
          <md>
<mrow>A = CDC\inv \sptxt{for} C = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix} \qquad D = \begin{pmatrix}
0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Alternatively, if we choose <m>2</m> as our first eigenvalue, then
          <md>
<mrow>A = C'D'(C')\inv \sptxt{for} C' = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 2 \\
-1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} \qquad D' = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      In the above example, the (non-invertible) matrix
      <m>A = \frac 13\smallmat{2}{-4}{-2}{4}</m>
      is similar to the diagonal matrix
      <m>D = \smallmat0002.</m>
      Since <m>A</m> is not invertible, zero is an eigenvalue by the <xref ref="imt-2" text="title">invertible matrix theorem</xref>, so one of the diagonal entries of <m>D</m> is necessarily zero.  Also see <xref ref="diag-eg-proj"/> below.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="diagonal-eg-33">
      <title>A diagonalizable <m>3\times 3</m> matrix</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Diagonalize the matrix
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
4 \amp -3 \amp 0 \\
2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
1 \amp -1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We need to find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of <m>A</m>.  First we compute the characteristic polynomial by expanding cofactors along the third column:
          <md>
<mrow>f(\lambda) \amp= \det(A-\lambda I_3)
            = (1-\lambda)\det\left(\begin{pmatrix}
4 \amp -3 \\
2 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix} - \lambda I_2\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= (1-\lambda)(\lambda^2 - 3\lambda + 2)
            = -(\lambda-1)^2(\lambda-2)</mrow>
</md>.
          Therefore, the eigenvalues are <m>1</m> and <m>2</m>.  We need to compute eigenvectors for each eigenvalue.  We start with <m>\lambda_1 = 1</m>:
          <md>
<mrow>(A-I_3)v = 0 \iff
\begin{pmatrix}
3 \amp -3 \amp 0 \\
2 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\
1 \amp -1 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}v = 0
  \;\xrightarrow{\text{RREF}}\;
\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}v = 0</mrow>
</md>.
          The parametric vector form is
          <md>
<mrow>\left\{\begin{alignedat}{2}
x \amp {}={} \amp y \amp {} \\
y \amp {}={} \amp y \amp {} \\
z \amp {}={} \amp z \amp {}
\end{alignedat}\right.
            \implies \begin{pmatrix}
x \\
y \\
z
\end{pmatrix} = y\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix} + z\begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Hence a basis for the <m>1</m>-eigenspace is
          <md>
<mrow>\cB_1 = \bigl\{ v_1,v_2 \bigr\} \sptxt{where}
v_1 = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix}, \quad v_2 = \begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Now we compute the eigenspace for <m>\lambda_2 = 2</m>:
          <md>
<mrow>(A-2I_3)v = 0 \iff
\begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp -3 \amp 0 \\
2 \amp -3 \amp 0 \\
1 \amp -1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix}v = 0
  \;\xrightarrow{\text{RREF}}\;
  \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp -3 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp -2 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}v = 0</mrow>
</md>
          The parametric form is <m>x = 3z, y = 2z</m>, so an eigenvector with eigenvalue <m>2</m> is
          <md>
<mrow>v_3 = \begin{pmatrix}
3 \\
2 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
        <p>
The eigenvectors <m>v_1,v_2,v_3</m> are linearly independent: <m>v_1,v_2</m> form a basis for the <m>1</m>-eigenspace, and <m>v_3</m> is not contained in the <m>1</m>-eigenspace because its eigenvalue is <m>2</m>.
          Therefore, the <xref ref="diagonalization-thm"/> says that
          <md>
<mrow>A = CDC\inv \sptxt{for} C = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\
1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}
            \qquad D = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
        <figure>
          <caption>The green plane is the <m>1</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m>, and the violet line is the <m>2</m>-eigenspace. There are three linearly independent eigenvectors visible in the picture: choose any two noncollinear vectors on the green plane, and any nonzero vector on the violet line.</caption>
          <interactive
              iframe="iframes/eigenspace-if.html?mat=4,-3,0:2,-1,0:1,-1,1&amp;nomult"
              width="90%"
              aspect="4:3">
            <shortdescription>
              <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </description>
            <instructions>
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </instructions>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      Here is the procedure we used in the above examples.
    </p>

    <algorithm>
      <title>Diagonalization</title>
      <idx><h>Diagonalizability</h><h>recipe</h></idx>
      <p>
        Let <m>A</m> be an <m>n\times n</m> matrix.  To diagonalize <m>A</m>:
        <ol>
          <li><p>
            Find the eigenvalues of <m>A</m> using the characteristic polynomial.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            For each eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m> of <m>A</m>, compute a basis <m>\cB_\lambda</m> for the <m>\lambda</m>-eigenspace.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            If there are fewer than <m>n</m> total vectors in all of the eigenspace bases <m>B_\lambda</m>, then the matrix is not diagonalizable.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            Otherwise, the <m>n</m> vectors <m>v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_n</m> in the eigenspace bases are linearly independent, and <m>A = CDC\inv</m> for
            <md>
<mrow>C = \begin{pmatrix}
| \amp | \amp | \\
v_1 \amp v_2 \amp \cdots \amp v_n \\
| \amp | \amp |
\end{pmatrix} \sptxt{and}
  D = \begin{pmatrix}
\lambda_1 \amp 0 \amp \cdots \amp 0 \\
0 \amp \lambda_2 \amp \cdots \amp 0 \\
\vdots \amp \vdots \amp \ddots \amp \vdots \\
0 \amp 0 \amp \cdots \amp \lambda_n
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>,
  where <m>\lambda_i</m> is the eigenvalue for <m>v_i</m>.
          </p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </algorithm>

    <p>
      We will justify the linear independence assertion in part 4 in the proof of <xref ref="diag-thm-variant"/> below.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="diag-eg-shear">
      <title>A shear is not diagonalizable</title>
      <idx><h>Diagonalizability</h><h>shear</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Shear</h><h>non-diagonalizability of</h></idx>
      <p>
        Let
        <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>,
        so <m>T(x) = Ax</m> is a <xref ref="matrix-trans-shear" text="title">shear</xref>.  The characteristic polynomial of <m>A</m> is <m>f(\lambda) = (\lambda-1)^2</m>, so the only eigenvalue of <m>A</m> is <m>1</m>.  We compute the <m>1</m>-eigenspace:
        <md>
<mrow>(A - I_2)v = 0 \iff
  \begin{pmatrix}
0 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
x \\
y
\end{pmatrix} = 0 \iff y = 0</mrow>
</md>.
        In other words, the <m>1</m>-eigenspace is exactly the <m>x</m>-axis, so <em>all</em> of the eigenvectors of <m>A</m> lie on the <m>x</m>-axis.  It follows that <m>A</m> does <em>not</em> admit two linearly independent eigenvectors, so by the <xref ref="diagonalization-thm"/>, it is not diagonalizable.
      </p>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="evecs-eg-shear"/>, we studied the eigenvalues of a shear geometrically; we reproduce the interactive demo here.
      </p>
        <figure>
          <caption>All eigenvectors of a shear lie on the <m>x</m>-axis.</caption>
          <interactive
              iframe="iframes/eigenspace-if.html?mat=1,1:0,1&amp;nomult"
              width="90%"
              aspect="4:3">
            <shortdescription>
              <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </description>
            <instructions>
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </instructions>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="diag-eg-proj">
      <title>A projection is diagonalizable</title>
      <idx><h>Diagonalizability</h><h>projection</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Projection</h><h>diagonalizability of</h></idx>
      <p>
        Let <m>L</m> be a line through the origin in <m>\R^2</m>, and define <m>T\colon\R^2\to\R^2</m> to be the transformation that sends a vector <m>x</m> to the closest point on <m>L</m> to <m>x</m>, as in the picture below.
        </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[thin border nodes, rotate=-15]
  \draw[seq-violet] (-3,-2) -- node[below right, very near start] {$L$} (3,2);
  \point[seq-red] (x) at (-3,2);
  \point (o) at (0,0);
  \draw[vector,seq-red] (o) -- node[auto,swap] {$x$} (x);
  \point[seq-blue, "$T(x)$" {below right,seq-blue}]
    (p) at (${-2.5/(1.5*1.5+1)}*(1.5,1)$);
  \draw[very thin, black!50] (p) -- (x);
  \pic[draw] {right angle=(x)--(p)--(o)};
\end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
</image>
<p>
        This is an example of an <em>orthogonal projection</em>.  We will see in <xref ref="sec-projections" text="type-global"/> that <m>T</m> is a linear transformation; let <m>A</m> be the matrix for <m>T</m>.  Any vector on <m>L</m> is not moved by <m>T</m> because it is the closest point on <m>L</m> to itself: hence it is an eigenvector of <m>A</m> with eigenvalue <m>1</m>.  Let <m>L^\perp</m> be the line perpendicular to <m>L</m> and passing through the origin.  Any vector <m>x</m> on <m>L^\perp</m> is closest to the zero vector on <m>L</m>, so a (nonzero) such vector is an eigenvector of <m>A</m> with eigenvalue <m>0</m>. (See <xref ref="evec-eg-projection"/> for a special case.)  Since <m>A</m> has two distinct eigenvalues, it is diagonalizable; in fact, we know from the <xref ref="diagonalization-thm"/> that <m>A</m> is similar to the matrix <m>\smallmat 1000</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Note that we never had to do any algebra!  We know that <m>A</m> is diagonalizable for <em>geometric</em> reasons.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <caption>The line <m>L</m> (violet) is the <m>1</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m>, and <m>L^\perp</m> (green) is the <m>0</m>-eigenspace.  Since there are linearly independent eigenvectors, we know that <m>A</m> is diagonalizable.</caption>
        <interactive
            iframe="iframes/eigenspace-if.html?mat=25/26,5/26:5/26,1/26&amp;nomult"
            width="90%"
            aspect="4:3">
          <shortdescription>
            <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
            <p>
              
            </p>
          </description>
          <instructions>
            <p>
              
            </p>
          </instructions>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="diag-eg-33-nondiag">
      <title>A non-diagonalizable <m>3\times 3</m> matrix</title>
      <p>
        Let
        <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        The characteristic polynomial of <m>A</m> is <m>f(\lambda) = -(\lambda-1)^2(\lambda-2)</m>, so the eigenvalues of <m>A</m> are <m>1</m> and <m>2</m>.  We compute the <m>1</m>-eigenspace:
        <md>
<mrow>(A - I_3)v = 0 \iff
  \begin{pmatrix}
0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
x \\
y \\
z
\end{pmatrix} = 0 \iff y = z = 0</mrow>
</md>.
        In other words, the <m>1</m>-eigenspace is the <m>x</m>-axis.  Similarly,
        <md>
<mrow>(A - 2I_3)v = 0 \iff
  \begin{pmatrix}
-1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
x \\
y \\
z
\end{pmatrix} = 0 \iff x = y = 0</mrow>
</md>,
        so the <m>2</m>-eigenspace is the <m>z</m>-axis.  In particular, all eigenvectors of <m>A</m> lie on the <m>xz</m>-plane, so there do not exist three linearly independent eigenvectors of <m>A</m>.  By the <xref ref="diagonalization-thm"/>, the matrix <m>A</m> is not diagonalizable.
      </p>
      <p>
        Notice that <m>A</m> contains a <m>2\times 2</m> block on its diagonal that looks like a shear:
        <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
 \amp 1 \amp  \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
 \amp 0 \amp  \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        This makes one suspect that such a matrix is not diagonalizable.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <caption>All eigenvectors of <m>A</m> lie on the <m>x</m>- and <m>z</m>-axes.</caption>
        <interactive
            iframe="iframes/eigenspace-if.html?mat=1,1,0:0,1,0:0,0,2&amp;nomult"
            width="90%"
            aspect="4:3">
          <shortdescription>
