      </figure>
    </observation>

    <p>
      In the following examples, we visualize the action of a diagonalizable matrix <m>A</m> in terms of its <em>dynamics</em>.  In other words, we start with a collection of vectors (drawn as points), and we see where they move when we multiply them by <m>A</m> repeatedly.
    </p>

    <example>
      <title>Eigenvalues <m>|\lambda_1| > 1,\,|\lambda_2|\lt1</m></title>
      <idx><h>Diagonalizability</h><h>dynamics of</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Describe how the matrix
          <md>
<mrow>A = \frac 1{10}\begin{pmatrix}
11 \amp 6 \\
9 \amp 14
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
          acts on the plane.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          First we diagonalize <m>A</m>.  The characteristic polynomial is
          <md>
<mrow>f(\lambda) = \lambda^2 - \Tr(A)\lambda + \det(A)
            = \lambda^2 - \frac 52\lambda + 1
            = (\lambda - 2)\left(\lambda - \frac 12\right)</mrow>
</md>.
          We compute the <m>2</m>-eigenspace:
          <md>
<mrow>(A-2I_3)v = 0 \iff
\frac 1{10}\begin{pmatrix}
-9 \amp 6 \\
9 \amp -6
\end{pmatrix}v = 0
  \;\xrightarrow{\text{RREF}}\;
\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp -2/3 \\
0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}v = 0</mrow>
</md>.
          The parametric form of this equation is <m>x = 2/3y</m>, so one eigenvector is <m>v_1 = {2/3\choose 1}</m>.  For the <m>1/2</m>-eigenspace, we have:
          <md>
<mrow>\left(A-\frac 12I_3\right)v = 0 \iff
\frac 1{10}\begin{pmatrix}
6 \amp 6 \\
9 \amp 9
\end{pmatrix}v = 0
  \;\xrightarrow{\text{RREF}}\;
\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}v = 0</mrow>
</md>.
          The parametric form of this equation is <m>x = -y</m>, so an eigenvector is <m>v_2 = {-1\choose 1}</m>.  It follows that <m>A = CDC\inv</m>, where
          <md>
<mrow>C = \begin{pmatrix}
2/3 \amp -1 \\
1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} \qquad D = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1/2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The diagonal matrix <m>D</m> scales the <m>x</m>-coordinate by <m>2</m> and the <m>y</m>-coordinate by <m>1/2</m>.  Therefore, it moves vectors closer to the <m>x</m>-axis and farther from the <m>y</m>-axis.  In fact, since <m>(2x)(y/2) = xy</m>, multiplication by <m>D</m> does not move a point off of a hyperbola <m>xy = C</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The matrix <m>A</m> does the same thing, in the <m>v_1,v_2</m>-coordinate system: multiplying a vector by <m>A</m> scales the <m>v_1</m>-coordinate by <m>2</m> and the <m>v_2</m>-coordinate by <m>1/2</m>.  Therefore, <m>A</m> moves vectors closer to the <m>2</m>-eigenspace and farther from the <m>1/2</m>-eigenspace.
        </p>
        <figure>
          <caption>Dynamics of the matrices <m>A</m> and <m>D</m>.  Click <q>multiply</q> to multiply the colored points by <m>D</m> on the left and <m>A</m> on the right.</caption>
          <interactive
              iframe="iframes/dynamics2-if.html?mat=2,0:0,1/2&amp;v1=2/3,1&amp;v2=-1,1&amp;y=1,5"
              width="90%"
              aspect="4:3">
            <shortdescription>
              <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </description>
            <instructions>
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </instructions>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="diag-l1big-l2big">
      <title>Eigenvalues <m>|\lambda_1| > 1,\,|\lambda_2| > 1</m></title>
      <idx><h>Diagonalizability</h><h>dynamics of</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Describe how the matrix
          <md>
<mrow>A = \frac 1{5}\begin{pmatrix}
13 \amp -2 \\
-3 \amp 12
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
          acts on the plane.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          First we diagonalize <m>A</m>.  The characteristic polynomial is
          <md>
<mrow>f(\lambda) = \lambda^2 - \Tr(A)\lambda + \det(A)
            = \lambda^2 - 5\lambda + 6
            = (\lambda - 2)(\lambda - 3)</mrow>
</md>.
          Next we compute the <m>2</m>-eigenspace:
          <md>
<mrow>(A-2I_3)v = 0 \iff
\frac 15\begin{pmatrix}
3 \amp -2 \\
-3 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix}v = 0
  \;\xrightarrow{\text{RREF}}\;
\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp -2/3 \\
0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}v = 0</mrow>
</md>.
          The parametric form of this equation is <m>x = 2/3y</m>, so one eigenvector is <m>v_1 = {2/3\choose 1}</m>.  For the <m>3</m>-eigenspace, we have:
          <md>
<mrow>(A-3I_3)v = 0 \iff
\frac 15\begin{pmatrix}
-2 \amp -2 \\
-3 \amp -3
\end{pmatrix}v = 0
  \;\xrightarrow{\text{RREF}}\;
\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}v = 0</mrow>
</md>.
          The parametric form of this equation is <m>x = -y</m>, so an eigenvector is <m>v_2 = {-1\choose 1}</m>.  It follows that <m>A = CDC\inv</m>, where
          <md>
<mrow>C = \begin{pmatrix}
2/3 \amp -1 \\
1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} \qquad D = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 3
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The diagonal matrix <m>D</m> scales the <m>x</m>-coordinate by <m>2</m> and the <m>y</m>-coordinate by <m>3</m>.  Therefore, it moves vectors farther from both the <m>x</m>-axis and the <m>y</m>-axis, but faster in the <m>y</m>-direction than the <m>x</m>-direction.
        </p>
        <p>
          The matrix <m>A</m> does the same thing, in the <m>v_1,v_2</m>-coordinate system: multiplying a vector by <m>A</m> scales the <m>v_1</m>-coordinate by <m>2</m> and the <m>v_2</m>-coordinate by <m>3</m>.  Therefore, <m>A</m> moves vectors farther from the <m>2</m>-eigenspace and the <m>3</m>-eigenspace, but faster in the <m>v_2</m>-direction than the <m>v_1</m>-direction.
        </p>
        <figure>
          <caption>Dynamics of the matrices <m>A</m> and <m>D</m>.  Click <q>multiply</q> to multiply the colored points by <m>D</m> on the left and <m>A</m> on the right.</caption>
          <interactive
              iframe="iframes/dynamics2-if.html?mat=2,0:0,3&amp;v1=2/3,1&amp;v2=-1,1&amp;y=2,1"
              width="90%"
              aspect="4:3">
            <shortdescription>
              <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </description>
            <instructions>
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </instructions>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <title>Eigenvalues <m>|\lambda_1| \lt 1,\,|\lambda_2| \lt 1</m></title>
      <idx><h>Diagonalizability</h><h>dynamics of</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Describe how the matrix
          <md>
<mrow>A' = \frac 1{30}\begin{pmatrix}
12 \amp 2 \\
3 \amp 13
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
          acts on the plane.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          This is the inverse of the matrix <m>A</m> from <xref ref="diag-l1big-l2big"/>.  In that example, we found <m>A = CDC\inv</m> for
          <md>
<mrow>C = \begin{pmatrix}
2/3 \amp -1 \\
1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} \qquad D = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 3
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Therefore, remembering that taking inverses <xref ref="matrix-inv-facts" text="title">reverses the order of multiplication</xref>, we have
          <md>
<mrow>A' = A\inv = (CDC\inv)\inv = (C\inv)\inv D\inv C\inv = C\begin{pmatrix}
1/2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1/3
\end{pmatrix}C\inv</mrow>
</md>.
          The diagonal matrix <m>D\inv</m> does the opposite of what <m>D</m> does: it scales the <m>x</m>-coordinate by <m>1/2</m> and the <m>y</m>-coordinate by <m>1/3</m>.  Therefore, it moves vectors closer to both coordinate axes, but faster in the <m>y</m>-direction.  The matrix <m>A'</m> does the same thing, but with respect to the <m>v_1,v_2</m>-coordinate system.
        </p>
        <figure>
          <caption>Dynamics of the matrices <m>A'</m> and <m>D\inv</m>.  Click <q>multiply</q> to multiply the colored points by <m>D\inv</m> on the left and <m>A'</m> on the right.</caption>
          <interactive
              iframe="iframes/dynamics2-if.html?mat=1/2,0:0,1/3&amp;v1=2/3,1&amp;v2=-1,1&amp;y=9,3"
              width="90%"
              aspect="4:3">
            <shortdescription>
              <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </description>
            <instructions>
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </instructions>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <title>Eigenvalues <m>|\lambda_1| = 1,\,|\lambda_2|\lt1</m></title>
      <idx><h>Diagonalizability</h><h>dynamics of</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Describe how the matrix
          <md>
<mrow>A = \frac 16\begin{pmatrix}
5 \amp -1 \\
-2 \amp 4
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
          acts on the plane.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          First we diagonalize <m>A</m>.  The characteristic polynomial is
          <md>
<mrow>f(\lambda) = \lambda^2 - \Tr(A)\lambda + \det(A)
            = \lambda^2 - \frac 32\lambda + \frac 12
            = (\lambda - 1)\left(\lambda - \frac 12\right)</mrow>
</md>.
          Next we compute the <m>1</m>-eigenspace:
          <md>
<mrow>(A-I_3)v = 0 \iff
\frac 16\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \amp -1 \\
-2 \amp -2
\end{pmatrix}v = 0
  \;\xrightarrow{\text{RREF}}\;
\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}v = 0</mrow>
</md>.
          The parametric form of this equation is <m>x = -y</m>, so one eigenvector is <m>v_1 = {-1\choose 1}</m>.  For the <m>1/2</m>-eigenspace, we have:
          <md>
<mrow>\left(A-\frac 12I_3\right)v = 0 \iff
\frac 16\begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp -1 \\
-2 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}v = 0
  \;\xrightarrow{\text{RREF}}\;
\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp -1/2 \\
0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}v = 0</mrow>
</md>.
          The parametric form of this equation is <m>x = 1/2y</m>, so an eigenvector is <m>v_2 = {1/2\choose 1}</m>.  It follows that <m>A = CDC\inv</m>, where
          <md>
<mrow>C = \begin{pmatrix}
-1 \amp 1/2 \\
1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} \qquad D = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1/2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The diagonal matrix <m>D</m> scales the <m>y</m>-coordinate by <m>1/2</m> and does not move the <m>x</m>-coordinate.  Therefore, it simply moves vectors closer to the <m>x</m>-axis along vertical lines.  The matrix <m>A</m> does the same thing, in the <m>v_1,v_2</m>-coordinate system: multiplying a vector by <m>A</m> scales the <m>v_2</m>-coordinate by <m>1/2</m> and does not change the <m>v_1</m>-coordinate.  Therefore, <m>A</m> <q>sucks vectors into the <m>1</m>-eigenspace</q> along lines parallel to <m>v_2</m>.
        </p>
        <figure>
          <caption>Dynamics of the matrices <m>A</m> and <m>D</m>.  Click <q>multiply</q> to multiply the colored points by <m>D</m> on the left and <m>A</m> on the right.</caption>
          <interactive
              iframe="iframes/dynamics2-if.html?mat=1,0:0,1/2&amp;v1=-1,1&amp;v2=1/2,1&amp;y=2,7"
              width="90%"
              aspect="4:3">
            <shortdescription>
              <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </description>
            <instructions>
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </instructions>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <question>
      <title>A diagonalizable <m>3\times 3</m> matrix</title>
      <p>
        The diagonal matrix
        <md>
<mrow>D = \begin{pmatrix}
1/2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 3/2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
        scales the <m>x</m>-coordinate by <m>1/2</m>, the <m>y</m>-coordinate by <m>2</m>, and the <m>z</m>-coordinate by <m>3/2</m>.  Looking straight down at the <m>xy</m>-plane, the points follow parabolic paths taking them away from the <m>x</m>-axis and toward the <m>y</m>-axis.  The <m>z</m>-coordinate is scaled by <m>3/2</m>, so points fly away from the <m>xy</m>-plane in that direction.
      </p>
      <p>
        If <m>A = CDC\inv</m> for some invertible matrix <m>C</m>, then <m>A</m> does the same thing as <m>D</m>, but with respect to the coordinate system defined by the columns of <m>C</m>.
      </p>
        <figure>
          <caption>Dynamics of the matrices <m>A</m> and <m>D</m>.  Click <q>multiply</q> to multiply the colored points by <m>D</m> on the left and <m>A</m> on the right.</caption>
          <interactive
              iframe="iframes/dynamics2-if.html?mat=1/2,0:0,2&amp;eigenz=3/2&amp;v1=-7/6,2/6,5/6&amp;v2=-1/6,-9/6,0&amp;v3=2/6,-1/6,3/6&amp;y=8,1,1"
              width="90%"
              aspect="4:3">
            <shortdescription>
              <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </description>
            <instructions>
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </instructions>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
    </question>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-algebraic-and-geometric-multiplicity">
    <title>Algebraic and Geometric Multiplicity</title>

    <p>
      In this subsection, we give a variant of the <xref ref="diagonalization-thm"/> that provides another criterion for diagonalizability.  It is stated in the language of <em>multiplicities</em> of eigenvalues.
    </p>

    <p>
      In algebra, we define the <em>multiplicity</em> of a root <m>\lambda_0</m> of a polynomial <m>f(\lambda)</m> to be the number of factors of <m>\lambda-\lambda_0</m> that divide <m>f(\lambda).</m>  For instance, in the polynomial
      <md>
<mrow>f(\lambda) = -\lambda^3 + 4\lambda^2 - 5\lambda + 2 = -(\lambda-1)^2(\lambda-2)</mrow>
</md>,
      the root <m>\lambda_0=2</m> has multiplicity <m>1</m>, and the root <m>\lambda_0=1</m> has multiplicity <m>2</m>.
    </p>

    <definition>
      <idx><h>Algebraic multiplicity</h><h>definition of</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Geometric multiplicity</h><h>definition of</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Multiplicity</h><h>algebraic</h><see>Algebraic multiplicity</see></idx>
      <idx><h>Multiplicity</h><h>geometric</h><see>Geometric multiplicity</see></idx>
      <idx><h>Eigenvalue</h><h>algebraic multiplicity of</h><see>Algebraic multiplicity</see></idx>
      <idx><h>Eigenvalue</h><h>geometric multiplicity of</h><see>Geometric multiplicity</see></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be an <m>n\times n</m> matrix, and let <m>\lambda</m> be an eigenvalue of <m>A</m>.
          <ol>
            <li><p>
              The <term>algebraic multiplicity</term> of <m>\lambda</m> is its multiplicity as a root of the characteristic polynomial of <m>A</m>.
            </p></li>
            <li><p>
              The <term>geometric multiplicity</term> of <m>\lambda</m> is the dimension of the <m>\lambda</m>-eigenspace.
            </p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Since the <m>\lambda</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m> is <m>\Nul(A-\lambda I_n)</m>, its dimension is the number of free variables in the system of equations <m>(A - \lambda I_n)x=0</m>, i.e., the number of columns without pivots in the matrix <m>A-\lambda I_n</m>.
    </p>

    <example>
      <idx><h>Shear</h><h>multiplicities</h></idx>
      <p>
        The shear matrix
        <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
        has only one eigenvalue <m>\lambda=1</m>.  The characteristic polynomial of <m>A</m> is <m>f(\lambda) = (\lambda-1)^2</m>, so <m>1</m> has algebraic multiplicity <m>2</m>, as it is a double root of <m>f</m>.  On the other hand, we showed in <xref ref="diag-eg-shear"/> that the <m>1</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m> is the <m>x</m>-axis, so the geometric multiplicity of <m>1</m> is equal to <m>1</m>.  This matrix is not diagonalizable.
      </p>
        <figure>
          <caption>Eigenspace of the shear matrix, with multiplicities.</caption>
          <interactive
              iframe="iframes/eigenspace-if.html?mat=1,1:0,1"
              width="90%"
              aspect="4:3">
            <shortdescription>
              <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </description>
            <instructions>
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </instructions>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      <p>
        The identity matrix
        <md>
<mrow>I_2 = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
        also has characteristic polynomial <m>(\lambda-1)^2</m>, so the eigenvalue <m>1</m> has algebraic multiplicity <m>2</m>.  Since every nonzero vector in <m>\R^2</m> is an eigenvector of <m>I_2</m> with eigenvalue <m>1</m>, the <m>1</m>-eigenspace is all of <m>\R^2</m>, so the geometric multiplicity is <m>2</m> as well.  This matrix is diagonal.
      </p>
        <figure>
          <caption>Eigenspace of the identity matrix, with multiplicities.</caption>
          <interactive
              iframe="iframes/eigenspace-if.html?mat=1,0:0,1"
              width="90%"
              aspect="4:3">
            <shortdescription>
              <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </description>
            <instructions>
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </instructions>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
    </example>

    <example>
      <p>
        Continuing with <xref ref="diagonal-eg-33"/>, let
        <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
4 \amp -3 \amp 0 \\
2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
1 \amp -1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        The characteristic polynomial is <m>f(\lambda) = -(\lambda-1)^2(\lambda-2)</m>, so that <m>1</m> and <m>2</m> are the eigenvalues, with algebraic multiplicities <m>2</m> and <m>1</m>, respectively.  We computed that the <m>1</m>-eigenspace is a plane and the <m>2</m>-eigenspace is a line, so that <m>1</m> and <m>2</m> also have geometric multiplicities <m>2</m> and <m>1</m>, respectively.  This matrix is diagonalizable.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <caption>The green plane is the <m>1</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m>, and the violet line is the <m>2</m>-eigenspace.  Hence the geometric multiplicity of the <m>1</m>-eigenspace is <m>2</m>, and the geometric multiplicity of the <m>2</m>-eigenspace is <m>1</m>.</caption>
        <interactive
            iframe="iframes/eigenspace-if.html?mat=4,-3,0:2,-1,0:1,-1,1"
            width="90%"
            aspect="4:3">
          <shortdescription>
            <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
            <p>
              
            </p>
          </description>
          <instructions>
            <p>
              
            </p>
          </instructions>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="diag-eg-33-nondiag"/>, we saw that the matrix
        <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
        also has characteristic polynomial <m>f(\lambda) = -(\lambda-1)^2(\lambda-2)</m>, so that <m>1</m> and <m>2</m> are the eigenvalues, with algebraic multiplicities <m>2</m> and <m>1</m>, respectively.  In this case, however, both eigenspaces are <em>lines,</em> so that both eigenvalues have geometric multiplicity <m>1</m>.  This matrix is not diagonalizable.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Both eigenspaces of <m>A</m> are lines, so they both have geometric multiplicity <m>1</m>.</caption>
        <interactive
            iframe="iframes/eigenspace-if.html?mat=1,1,0:0,1,0:0,0,2"
            width="90%"
            aspect="4:3">
          <shortdescription>
            <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
            <p>
              
            </p>
          </description>
          <instructions>
            <p>
              
            </p>
          </instructions>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </example>

    <p>
      We saw in the above examples that the algebraic and geometric multiplicities need not coincide.  However, they do satisfy the following fundamental inequality, the proof of which is beyond the scope of this text.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="diag-amgm">
      <title>Algebraic and Geometric Multiplicity</title>
      <idx><h>Algebraic multiplicity</h><h>and geometric multiplicity</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Geometric multiplicity</h><h>and algebraic multiplicity</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be a square matrix and let <m>\lambda</m> be an eigenvalue of <m>A</m>.  Then
          <md>
<mrow>1 \leq \text{(the geometric multiplicity of $\lambda$)}
  \leq \text{(the algebraic multiplicity of $\lambda$)}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <p>
      In particular, if the algebraic multiplicity of <m>\lambda</m> is equal to <m>1</m>, then so is the geometric multiplicity.
    </p>

    <insight>
      <idx><h>Algebraic multiplicity</h><h>equals one</h></idx>
      <p>
        If <m>A</m> has an eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m> with algebraic multiplicity <m>1</m>, then the <m>\lambda</m>-eigenspace is a <em>line.</em>
      </p>
    </insight>

    <p>
      We can use the <xref ref="diag-amgm"/> to give another criterion for diagonalizability (in addition to the <xref ref="diagonalization-thm"/>).
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="diag-thm-variant">
      <title>Diagonalization Theorem, Variant</title>
      <idx><h>Algebraic multiplicity</h><h>and diagonalizability</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Geometric multiplicity</h><h>and diagonalizability</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Diagonalizability</h><h>algebraic-geometric multiplicity criterion</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be an <m>n\times n</m> matrix.  The following are equivalent:
          <ol>
            <li><p>
              <m>A</m> is diagonalizable.
            </p></li>
            <li><p>
              The sum of the geometric multiplicities of the eigenvalues of <m>A</m> is equal to <m>n</m>.
            </p></li>
            <li><p>
              The sum of the algebraic multiplicities of the eigenvalues of <m>A</m> is equal to <m>n</m>, and for each eigenvalue, the geometric multiplicity equals the algebraic multiplicity.
            </p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          We will show <m>1\implies 2\implies 3\implies 1</m>.  First suppose that <m>A</m> is diagonalizable.  Then <m>A</m> has <m>n</m> linearly independent eigenvectors <m>v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_n</m>.  This implies that the sum of the geometric multiplicities is <em>at least</em> <m>n</m>: for instance, if <m>v_1,v_2,v_3</m> have the same eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m>, then the geometric multiplicity of <m>\lambda</m> is at least <m>3</m> (as the <m>\lambda</m>-eigenspace contains three linearly independent vectors), and so on.  But the sum of the algebraic multiplicities is greater than or equal to the sum of the geometric multiplicities by the <xref ref="diag-amgm"/>, and the sum of the algebraic multiplicities is at most <m>n</m> because the characteristic polynomial has degree <m>n</m>.  Therefore, the sum of the geometric multiplicities equals <m>n</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now suppose that the sum of the geometric multiplicities equals <m>n</m>.  As above, this forces the sum of the algebraic multiplicities to equal <m>n</m> as well.  As the algebraic multiplicities are all greater than or equal to the geometric multiplicities in any case, this implies that they are in fact equal.
        </p>
        <p>
          Finally, suppose that the third condition is satisfied.  Then the sum of the geometric multiplicities equals <m>n</m>.  Suppose that the distinct eigenvectors are <m>\lambda_1,\lambda_2,\ldots,\lambda_k</m>, and that <m>\cB_i</m> is a basis for the <m>\lambda_i</m>-eigenspace, which we call <m>V_i</m>.  We claim that the collection <m>\cB = \{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_n\}</m> of all vectors in all of the eigenspace bases <m>\cB_i</m> is linearly independent.  Consider the vector equation
          <md>
<mrow>0 = c_1v_1 + c_2v_2 + \cdots + c_nv_n</mrow>
</md>.
          Grouping the eigenvectors with the same eigenvalues, this sum has the form
          <md>
<mrow>0 = \text{(something in $V_1$)} + \text{(something in $V_2$)} + \cdots + \text{(something in $V_k$)}</mrow>
</md>.
          Since <xref ref="evecs-linindep" text="title">eigenvectors with distinct eigenvalues are linearly independent</xref>, each <q>something in <m>V_i</m></q> is equal to zero.  But this implies that all coefficients <m>c_1,c_2,\ldots,c_n</m> are equal to zero, since the vectors in each <m>\cB_i</m> are linearly independent.  Therefore, <m>A</m> has <m>n</m> linearly independent eigenvectors, so it is diagonalizable.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <p>
      The first part of the third statement simply says that the characteristic polynomial of <m>A</m> factors completely into linear polynomials over the real numbers: in other words, there are no complex (non-real) roots.  The second part of the third statement says in particular that for any diagonalizable matrix, the algebraic and geometric multiplicities coincide.
    </p>

    <insight>
      <p>
        Let <m>A</m> be a square matrix and let <m>\lambda</m> be an eigenvalue of <m>A</m>.  If the algebraic multiplicity of <m>\lambda</m> does not equal the geometric multiplicity, then <m>A</m> is not diagonalizable.
      </p>
    </insight>

    <p>
      The examples at the beginning of this subsection illustrate the theorem.  Here we give some general consequences for diagonalizability of <m>2\times 2</m> and <m>3\times 3</m> matrices.
    </p>

    <observation xml:id="diag-cases-22">
      <title>Diagonalizability of <m>2\times 2</m> Matrices</title>
      <idx><h>Diagonalizability</h><h>of <m>2\times 2</m> matrices</h></idx>
      <p>
        Let <m>A</m> be a <m>2\times 2</m> matrix.  There are four cases:
        <ol>
          <li><p>
            <m>A</m> has two different eigenvalues.  In this case, each eigenvalue has algebraic and geometric multiplicity equal to one.  This implies <m>A</m> is diagonalizable.  For example:
            <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 7 \\
0 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            <m>A</m> has one eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m> of algebraic and geometric multiplicity <m>2</m>.  To say that the geometric multiplicity is <m>2</m> means that <m>\Nul(A-\lambda I_2) = \R^2</m>, i.e., that every vector in <m>\R^2</m> is in the null space of <m>A-\lambda I_2</m>.  This implies that <m>A-\lambda I_2</m> is the zero matrix, so that <m>A</m> is the diagonal matrix <m>\lambda I_2</m>.  In particular, <m>A</m> is diagonalizable.  For example:
            <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            <m>A</m> has one eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m> of algebraic multiplicity <m>2</m> and geometric multiplicity <m>1</m>.  In this case, <m>A</m> is not diagonalizable, by part 3 of the <xref ref="diag-thm-variant"/>.  For example, a <xref ref="diag-eg-shear" text="title">shear</xref>:
            <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            <m>A</m> has no eigenvalues.  This happens when the characteristic polynomial has no real roots.  In particular, <m>A</m> is not diagonalizable.  For example, a <xref ref="diag-eg-rotation" text="title">rotation</xref>:
            <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp -1 \\
1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          </p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </observation>

    <example xml:id="diag-cases-33">
      <title>Diagonalizability of <m>3\times 3</m> Matrices</title>
      <idx><h>Diagonalizability</h><h>of <m>3\times 3</m> matrices</h></idx>
      <p>
        Let <m>A</m> be a <m>3\times 3</m> matrix.  We can analyze the diagonalizability of <m>A</m> on a case-by-case basis, as in <xref ref="diag-cases-22"/>.
        <ol>
          <li><p>
            <m>A</m> has three different eigenvalues.  In this case, each eigenvalue has algebraic and geometric multiplicity equal to one.  This implies <m>A</m> is diagonalizable.  For example:
            <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 7 \amp 4 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 3 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            <m>A</m> has two distinct eigenvalues <m>\lambda_1,\lambda_2</m>.  In this case, one has algebraic multiplicity one and the other has algebraic multiplicity two; after reordering, we can assume <m>\lambda_1</m> has multiplicity <m>1</m> and <m>\lambda_2</m> has multiplicity <m>2</m>.  This implies that <m>\lambda_1</m> has geometric multiplicity <m>1</m>, so <em><m>A</m> is diagonalizable if and only if the <m>\lambda_2</m>-eigenspace is a plane.</em>  For example:
            <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 7 \amp 4 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
            On the other hand, if the geometric multiplicity of <m>\lambda_2</m> is <m>1</m>, then <m>A</m> is not diagonalizable.  For example:
            <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 7 \amp 4 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            <m>A</m> has only one eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m>.  If the algebraic multiplicity of <m>\lambda</m> is <m>1</m>, then <m>A</m> is not diagonalizable.  This happens when the characteristic polynomial has two complex (non-real) roots.  For example:
            <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
            Otherwise, the algebraic multiplicity of <m>\lambda</m> is equal to <m>3</m>.  In this case, if the geometric multiplicity is <m>1</m>:
            <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
            or <m>2</m>:
            <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
            then <m>A</m> is not diagonalizable.  If the geometric multiplicity is <m>3</m>, then <m>\Nul(A-\lambda I_3) = \R^3</m>, so that <m>A-\lambda I_3</m> is the zero matrix, and hence <m>A = \lambda I_3</m>.  Therefore, in this case <m>A</m> is necessarily diagonal, as in:
            <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          </p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </example>

    <paragraphs component="default">
      <title>Similarity and multiplicity</title>
    <p>
      Recall from <xref ref="similarity-charpoly"/> that similar matrices have the same eigenvalues.  It turns out that <em>both</em> notions of multiplicity of an eigenvalue are preserved under similarity.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="diag-similar-amgm">
      <idx><h>Similarity</h><h>and multiplicities</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Algebraic multiplicity</h><h>of similar matrices</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Geometric multiplicity</h><h>of similar matrices</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> be similar <m>n\times n</m> matrices, and let <m>\lambda</m> be an eigenvalue of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.  Then:
          <ol>
            <li><p>
              The algebraic multiplicity of <m>\lambda</m> is the same for <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.
            </p></li>
            <li><p>
              The geometric multiplicity of <m>\lambda</m> is the same for <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.
            </p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Since <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> have the same characteristic polynomial, the multiplicity of <m>\lambda</m> as a root of the characteristic polynomial is the same for both matrices, which proves the first statement.  For the second, suppose that <m>A = CBC\inv</m> for an invertible matrix <m>C</m>.  By <xref ref="similarity-evecs"/>, the matrix <m>C</m> takes eigenvectors of <m>B</m> to eigenvectors of <m>A</m>, both with eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Let <m>\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_k\}</m> be a basis of the <m>\lambda</m>-eigenspace of <m>B</m>.  We claim that <m>\{Cv_1,Cv_2,\ldots,Cv_k\}</m> is linearly independent.  Suppose that
          <md>
<mrow>c_1Cv_1 + c_2Cv_2 + \cdots + c_kCv_k = 0</mrow>
</md>.
          Regrouping, this means
          <md>
<mrow>C\bigl(c_1v_1 + c_2v_2 + \cdots + c_kv_k\bigr) = 0</mrow>
</md>.
          By the <xref ref="imt-2"/>, the null space of <m>C</m> is trivial, so this implies
          <md>
<mrow>c_1v_1 + c_2v_2 + \cdots + c_kv_k = 0</mrow>
</md>.
          Since <m>v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_k</m> are linearly independent, we get <m>c_1=c_2=\cdots=c_k=0</m>, as desired.
        </p>
        <p>
          By the previous paragraph, the dimension of the <m>\lambda</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m> is greater than or equal to the dimension of the <m>\lambda</m>-eigenspace of <m>B</m>.  By symmetry (<m>B</m> is similar to <m>A</m> as well), the dimensions are equal, so the geometric multiplicities coincide.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <p>
      For instance, the four matrices in <xref ref="diag-cases-22"/> are not similar to each other, because the algebraic and/or geometric multiplicities of the eigenvalues do not match up.  Or, combined with the above <xref ref="diag-thm-variant"/>, we see that a diagonalizable matrix cannot be similar to a non-diagonalizable one, because the algebraic and geometric multiplicities of such matrices cannot both coincide.
    </p>

    <example>
      <p>
        Continuing with <xref ref="diagonal-eg-33"/>, let
        <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
4 \amp -3 \amp 0 \\
2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
1 \amp -1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        This is a diagonalizable matrix that is similar to
        <md>
<mrow>D = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix}
        \sptxt{using the matrix}
          C = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\
1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        The <m>1</m>-eigenspace of <m>D</m> is the <m>xy</m>-plane, and the <m>2</m>-eigenspace is the <m>z</m>-axis.  The matrix <m>C</m> takes the <m>xy</m>-plane to the <m>1</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m>, which is again a plane, and the <m>z</m>-axis to the <m>2</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m>, which is again a line.  This shows that the geometric multiplicities of <m>A</m> and <m>D</m> coincide.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <caption>The matrix <m>C</m> takes the <m>xy</m>-plane to the <m>1</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m> (the grid) and the <m>z</m>-axis to the <m>2</m>-eigenspace (the green line).</caption>
        <interactive
            iframe="iframes/similarity-if.html?C=1,0,3:1,0,2:0,1,1&amp;B=1,0,0:0,1,0:0,0,2"
            width="90%"
            aspect="4:3">
          <shortdescription>
            <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
            <p>
              
            </p>
          </description>
          <instructions>
            <p>
              
            </p>
          </instructions>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </example>

    <p>
      The converse of the <xref ref="diag-similar-amgm"/> is false: there exist matrices  whose eigenvectors have the same algebraic and geometric multiplicities, but which are not similar.  See the <xref ref="diag-eg-nonsimilar"/> below.
      However, for <m>2\times 2</m> and <m>3\times 3</m> matrices whose characteristic polynomial has no complex (non-real) roots, the converse of the <xref ref="diag-similar-amgm"/> is true.  (We will handle the case of complex roots in <xref ref="sec-complex-eigenvalues" text="type-global"/>.)
    </p>

    <example xml:id="diag-eg-nonsimilar">
      <title>Matrices that look similar but are not</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that the matrices
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix} \sptxt{and}
            B = \begin{pmatrix}
0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
          have the same eigenvalues with the same algebraic and geometric multiplicities, but are not similar.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          These matrices are upper-triangular.  They both have characteristic polynomial <m>f(\lambda) = \lambda^4</m>, so they both have one eigenvalue <m>0</m> with algebraic multiplicity <m>4</m>.  The <m>0</m>-eigenspace <xref ref="evecs-eval0" text="title">is the null space</xref>, which has dimension <m>2</m> in each case because <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> have two columns without pivots.  Hence <m>0</m> has geometric multiplicity <m>2</m> in each case.
        </p>
        <p>
          To show that <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are not similar, we note that
          <md>
<mrow>A^2 = \begin{pmatrix}
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}
          \sptxt{and} B^2 = \begin{pmatrix}
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>,
          as the reader can verify.  If <m>A = CBC\inv</m> then by <xref ref="diag-powers"/>, we have
          <md>
<mrow>A^2 = CB^2C\inv = C0C\inv = 0</mrow>
</md>,
          which is not the case.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      On the other hand, suppose that <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are <em>diagonalizable</em> matrices with the same characteristic polynomial.  Since the geometric multiplicities of the eigenvalues coincide with the algebraic multiplicities, which are the same for <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, we conclude that there exist <m>n</m> linearly independent eigenvectors of each matrix, all of which have the same eigenvalues.  This shows that <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are both similar to the same diagonal matrix.  Using  the <xref ref="similarity-eq-reln" text="title">transitivity property</xref> of similar matrices, this shows:
    </p>

    <insight>
      <idx><h>Similarity</h><h>of diagonalizable matrices</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Diagonalizability</h><h>criterion for similarity</h></idx>
      <p>
        <em>Diagonalizable</em> matrices are similar if and only if they have the same characteristic polynomial, or equivalently, the same eigenvalues with the same algebraic multiplicities.
      </p>
    </insight>

    <example xml:id="diag-similarity">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that the matrices
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 7 \amp 2 \\
0 \amp -1 \amp 3 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 4
\end{pmatrix} \sptxt{and}
            B = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
-2 \amp 4 \amp 0 \\
-5 \amp -4 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
          are similar.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Both matrices have the three distinct eigenvalues <m>1,-1,4</m>.  Hence they are both diagonalizable, and are similar to the diagonal matrix
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 4
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          By the <xref ref="similarity-eq-reln" text="title">transitivity property</xref> of similar matrices, this implies that <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are similar to each other.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <title>Diagonal matrices with the same entries are similar</title>
      <idx><h>Similarity</h><h>of diagonal matrices</h></idx>
      <p>
        Any two diagonal matrices with the same diagonal entries (possibly in a different order) are similar to each other.  Indeed, such matrices have the same characteristic polynomial.  We saw this phenomenon in <xref ref="diag-easy-eg"/>, where we noted that
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 3
\end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
1 \amp 0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
3 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}
            \begin{pmatrix}
0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
1 \amp 0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}\inv</mrow>
</md>.
      </p>
    </example>

  </paragraphs>


  </subsection>
<exercises>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="yes">
      <p>If <m>A</m> is a <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix with eigenvalues <m>0</m>, <m>1</m>, and <m>2</m>, then
      <m>A</m> is diagonalizable.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>True: any <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix with <m>3</m> real eigenvalues is automatically
        diagonalizable.  One way to see this is that each of the <m>3</m> eigenvalues must
        have a nonzero eigenvector in their eigenspace, and eigenvectors corresponding
        to different eigenvalues are linearly independent, therefore we obtain <m>3</m>
        linearly independent eigenvectors.  In other words, we obtain a basis for
        <m>{\bf R}^{3}</m> consisting of eigenvectors of <m>A</m>, which is equivalent to the
        property that <m>A</m> is diagonalizable.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="no">
      <p>If <m>\lambda</m> is an eigenvalue of a matrix <m>A</m>, it is possible
      that the <m>\lambda</m>-eigenspace is <m>0</m>-dimensional.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>False.  It is a fundamental fact that the geometric multiplicity of any
        eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m> is at least <m>1</m>.  By definition of eigenvalue, the
        equation <m>Ax = \lambda x</m> must have a non-trivial solution, therefore
        <m>(A-\lambda I)x = 0</m> has a non-trivial solution, which means that
        <m>\Nul(A-\lambda I)</m> is at least <m>1</m>-dimensional.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="no">
      <p>All diagonalizable matrices are invertible.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>False: for example, zero matrix is diagonalizable (in fact, diagonal!) but is
        not invertible.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="no">
      <p>All invertible matrices are diagonalizable.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>False: for example, take <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</m>. Note <m>\det(A) = 1</m>, so <m>A</m> is
        invertible.  However, the only eigenvalue of <m>A</m> is <m>\lambda = 1</m>, and we can
        compute that the <m>1</m>-eigenspace is only <m>1</m>-dimensional, so <m>A</m> is not
        diagonalizable.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="yes">
      <p>All diagonal <m>n \times n</m> matrices are diagonalizable.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>True: if <m>A</m> is diagonal, then <m>A = I A I^{-1}</m>, so <m>A</m> is diagonalizable.  In
        other words, <m>A = CDC^{-1}</m> where <m>C</m> is the <m>n \times n</m> identity matrix and
        <m>D</m> is <m>A</m> itself!</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="no">
      <p>All upper-triangular <m>n \times n</m> matrices are diagonalizable.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        False: for example,
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        The only eigenvalue of <m>A</m> is <m>\lambda = 2</m>, but we can
        compute that the <m>2</m>-eigenspace is only <m>1</m>-dimensional, so <m>A</m> is not
        diagonalizable.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Find <m>\begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 6 \\ 0 \amp -1\end{pmatrix}^{100}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <m>A</m> is <m>2 \times 2</m> with <m>2</m> different eigenvalues <m>\lambda = 2</m> and
          <m>\lambda = -1</m>, so <m>A</m> is diagonalizable.
        <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{r|r}A-2I \amp 0\end{array}\right) = \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}0 \amp 6 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -3 \amp 0\end{array}\right) \xrightarrow[]{RREF}
            \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right), \quad 2\textup{-eigenspace}
            = \Span \left\{\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} \right\}</mrow></md>.
        <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{r|r}A+I \amp 0\end{array}\right) = \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}3 \amp 6 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right) \xrightarrow[]{RREF}
            \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right), \quad (-1)\textup{-eigenspace}
            = \Span \left\{\begin{pmatrix}-2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} \right\}</mrow></md>.
        Therefore, <m>A = CDC^{-1}</m> where <m>C = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp -2 \\ 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</m> and
          <m>D = \begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1\end{pmatrix}</m>.  Using the repeated cancellations of <m>C</m> with
          <m>C^{-1}</m> when expanding <m>A^{100}</m>, we get
        <md>
          <mrow>A^{100} \amp  = (C D C^{-1})^{100} = C D^{100} C^{-1}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = C \begin{pmatrix}2^{100} \amp 0</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp (-1)^{100}\end{pmatrix} C^{-1}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp -2</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}2^{100} \amp 0</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 2</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp -2</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}2^{100} \amp 2^{101}</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = \begin{pmatrix}2^{100} \amp 2^{101}-2</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Let <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \amp 0 \amp -2 \\ 0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 3 \amp 0 \amp 4\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Find the eigenvalues of <m>A</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We solve <m>0=\det(A-\lambda I)</m>.
          <md>
            <mrow>0 \amp  = \det\begin{pmatrix}-1-\lambda \amp 0 \amp -2</mrow>
            <mrow>0 \amp 2-\lambda \amp 0</mrow>
            <mrow>3 \amp 0 \amp 4-\lambda\end{pmatrix} =
                    (2-\lambda)(-1)^{4} \det\begin{pmatrix}-1-\lambda \amp -2</mrow>
            <mrow>3 \amp 4-\lambda\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp  =(2-\lambda)\left((-1-\lambda)(4-\lambda) + 6\right)
                     = (2-\lambda)(\lambda^2-3\lambda -4+6)</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp  = (2-\lambda)(\lambda^2-3\lambda+2)
                     = (2-\lambda)(\lambda-2)(\lambda-1)</mrow>
          </md>
          So <m>\lambda = 1</m> and <m>\lambda = 2</m> are the eigenvalues.
        </p>
        <p>\underline{<m>\lambda = 1</m>}:
            <m>\left(\begin{array}{r|r}A-I \amp 0\end{array}\right) = \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}-2 \amp 0 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 3 \amp 0 \amp 3 \amp 0\end{array}\right)
            \xrightarrow[\textup{then } R_1 = -R_1
            /2]{R_3=R_3+\frac{3}{2}R_1}\left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)</m> with
            solution <m>x_1= -x_3</m>, <m>x_2=0</m>, <m>x_3=x_3</m>.  The <m>1</m>-eigenspace has
            basis <m>\left\{ \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} \right\}</m>.</p>
        <p>
          \underline{<m>\lambda = 2</m>}:
          <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{r|r}A-2I \amp 0\end{array}\right) = \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}-3 \amp 0 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 3 \amp 0 \amp 2 \amp 0\end{array}\right)
                  \xrightarrow[\textup{then } R_1= -R_1/3]{R_3=R_3+R_1} \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{2}{3} \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)</mrow></md>
          with solution
                <m>x_1= -\frac{2}{3}x_3</m>, \ <m>x_2=x_2</m>, \ <m>x_3=x_3</m>.  The
                <m>2</m>-eigenspace has basis
                <m>\left\{ \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix}-2/3 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Find a basis for each eigenspace of <m>A</m>.  Mark your
          answers clearly.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p><m>A</m> is diagonalizable; <m>A=PDP^{-1}</m> where
              <m>P = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \amp 0 \amp -2/3 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</m> and
              <m>D= \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>\enspace Is <m>A</m> diagonalizable?  If your answer is yes,
          find a diagonal matrix <m>D</m> and an invertible matrix <m>P</m> so that
          <m>A = PDP^{-1}</m>.  If your answer is no, justify why <m>A</m> is not
          diagonalizable.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Let <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 4 \amp 6 \\ 0 \amp 2 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 4\end{pmatrix}</m>.  Is <m>A</m> diagonalizable?
      If so, find a basis of <m>{\bf R}^3</m> consisting of eigenvectors of <m>A</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
      <ol marker="(a)">
        <li>
          <p>The matrix is upper-triangular, so the eigenvalues are
            the diagonal entries: <m>\lambda_1 = -1</m>, <m>\lambda_2 = 4</m>, and
            <m>\lambda_3 = 5</m>.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            We find a basis for each eigenspace.
            <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{r|r}A-\lambda_1I \amp 0\end{array}\right) =
                \left(\begin{array}{r|r}A+I \amp 0\end{array}\right) =
                \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}0 \amp 5 \amp 10 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 5 \amp 10 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 6 \amp 0\end{array}\right)
                \xrightarrow[]{RREF}
                \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)</mrow></md>.
            This means <m>x_2 = x_3 = 0</m>, so a basis for <m>(-1)</m>-eigenspace
              is <m>\left\{\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>.
            <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{r|r}A-\lambda_2I \amp 0\end{array}\right) =
                \left(\begin{array}{r|r}A-4I \amp 0\end{array}\right) =
                \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}-5 \amp 5 \amp 10 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 10 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0\end{array}\right)
                \xrightarrow[]{RREF}
                \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)</mrow></md>.
            So <m>x_1 = x_2</m> (with <m>x_2</m> free) and <m>x_3 = 0</m>, so
              a basis for the <m>4</m>-eigenspace is <m>\left\{\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{r|r}A-\lambda_3I \amp 0\end{array}\right) =
                \left(\begin{array}{r|r}A-5I \amp 0\end{array}\right) = \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}-6 \amp 5 \amp 10 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \amp 10 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)
                \xrightarrow[]{RREF} \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}1 \amp 0 \amp -10 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -10 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)</mrow></md>.
            Thus
              <m>x_1 = 10x_3</m> and <m>x_2 = 10x_3</m> (<m>x_3</m> free). Basis for
              <m>5</m>-eigenspace is <m>\left\{ \begin{pmatrix}10 \\ 10 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} \right\}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Yes.  We form <m>C</m> using the three eigenvectors we found
              in part (b), and <m>D</m> is the diagonal matrix with corresponding
              eigenvalues.
            <md><mrow>C = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 1 \amp 10 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 10 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix} \qquad
                D = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 4 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 5\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Write a <m>4 \times 4</m> matrix <m>A</m> with only one real eigenvalue <m>\lambda = 2</m>, so
      that the <m>2</m>-eigenspace for <m>A</m> is a three-dimensional subspace of <m>{\bf
        R}^4</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We need <m>\lambda=2</m> to be the only eigenvalue, and we need <m>\left(\begin{array}{r|r}A-2I \amp 0\end{array}\right)</m> to
          have three free variables and thus one pivot.  For example,
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Write a <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix <m>B</m> with the property that <m>e_1</m>, <m>e_2</m>, and <m>e_3</m>
      are eigenvectors of <m>B</m>.  Must <m>B</m> be diagonalizable?  Justify your answer.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Endless possibilities.  For example,
        <md><mrow>B = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}, \qquad B = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 5 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp -3\end{pmatrix}, \qquad
          B = \begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 3 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 3\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        Note that <m>B</m> must be diagonalizable because
        it is a <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix with <m>3</m> linearly independent eigenvectors, namely
        <m>e_1</m>, <m>e_2</m>, <m>e_3</m>.  In truth, <m>B</m> satisfies the conditions of this problem if
        and only if it is a diagonal <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Find the <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix <m>A</m> whose eigenspaces are drawn below.
      Fully simplify your answer.</p>
      <p>(to be clear: the dashed line is the <m>(-2)</m>-eigenspace).</p>
      <image source="images/e3_pic1">
        <shortdescription><!-- FILL: concise image description --></shortdescription>
        <description><p><!-- FILL: complete image description --></p></description>
      </image>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        From the picture, we see <m>\lambda_1 = 1</m> has eigenvector
          <m>v_1 = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m>.  Also, <m>\lambda= -2</m> has eigenvector
          <m>v_2 = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m>.  Forming <m>P=\begin{pmatrix}v_1 \amp v_2\end{pmatrix}</m> and
          <m>D=\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -2\end{pmatrix}</m> we get <m>A=PDP^{-1}</m>, so
        <md>
          <mrow>A \amp  = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp -1</mrow>
          <mrow>2 \amp 1\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp -2\end{pmatrix} \dfrac{1}{3}
             \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>-2 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = \frac{1}{3} \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp -1</mrow>
          <mrow>2 \amp 1\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>4 \amp -2\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = \frac{1}{3} \begin{pmatrix}-3 \amp 3</mrow>
          <mrow>6 \amp 0\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>2 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Let <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 3 \\ 18 \amp 2\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}-2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 3\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp -5 \\ 18 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}^{-1}</m>.</p>
      <p>
        Find all values of <m>k</m> (if there are any) so that
        <md><mrow>A \begin{pmatrix}9 \\ k\end{pmatrix}= \begin{pmatrix}27 \\ 3k\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>We do not need to do any matrix multiplication, since <m>A</m> has been
        diagonalized for us:</p>
      <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p><m>\lambda = -2</m> is an eigenvalue with corresponding eigenspace
              Span<m>\left\{\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 18\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><m>\lambda = 3</m> is an eigenvalue with corresponding eigenspace
              Span<m>\left\{\begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m></p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      </p>
      <p>The problem is subtly asking us to find <m>k</m> so that <m>\begin{pmatrix}9 \\ k\end{pmatrix}</m> is
        in the <m>3</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m>.  This means <m>\begin{pmatrix}9 \\ k\end{pmatrix}</m> is a scalar
        multiple of <m>\begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m>, so <m>k = 6</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose <m>A</m> is a diagonalizable <m>n \times n</m> matrix, and
          suppose <m>B</m> is an <m>n \times n</m> matrix with the same eigenvalues of
          <m>A</m> and the same corresponding eigenspaces for each eigenvalue.  Is
          it necessarily true that <m>A=B</m>?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Yes.  Since <m>A</m> is diagonalizable, we can write
            <m>A = PDP^{-1}</m>, where the columns of <m>P</m> are a basis of
            <m>{\bf R}^n</m> consisting of eigenvectors of <m>A</m> and where <m>D</m> is the
            diagonal matrix with the corresponding eigenvalues.  Since <m>B</m> has
            the same eigenvalues of <m>A</m> and the same corresponding
            eigenspaces, we also have <m>B = PDP^{-1}</m>, so <m>A = B = PDP^{-1}</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Answer part (a) in the case that <m>A</m> is an <m>n \times n</m>
          matrix which is \textit{not} diagonalizable.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>No.  For example, consider <m>A= \begin{pmatrix}0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}</m> and
            <m>B = \begin{pmatrix}0 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}</m>.  Each matrix has only one eigenvalue
            <m>\lambda = 0</m> with algebraic multiplicity <m>2</m>, and the
            <m>0</m>-eigenspace for each matrix is the span of <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}</m>, yet
            <m>A \neq B</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the following matrices <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}3 \amp 1 \amp 4 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ -1 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}\sqrt{2} \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 70^{1/7} \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp \pi\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}3 \amp 1 \amp 4 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ -1 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}^{-1}</mrow></md>,
        <md><mrow>B = \begin{pmatrix}-3 \amp 3 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 3 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{1}{2}\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}\sqrt{2} \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 70^{1/7} \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp \pi\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}-3 \amp 3 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 3 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{1}{2}\end{pmatrix}^{-1}</mrow></md>.
        Without doing any computations, determine whether <m>A = B</m> or
        <m>A \neq B</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>We show that <m>A=B</m>.  By the Diagonalization Theorem, <m>A = PDP^{-1}</m>,
        where <m>D</m> is any diagonal matrix of eigenvalues of <m>A</m> (with their
        geometric multiplicities) and <m>P</m> is \textit{any} matrix whose
        column vectors form bases for the corresponding eigenspaces.</p>
      <p>The <m>\sqrt{2}</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m> is
        <m>\textup{Span}\left\{\begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 0 \\ -1\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>, which is the same as
        <m>\textup{Span}\left\{\begin{pmatrix}-3 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>.</p>
      <p>The <m>70^{1/7}</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m> is <m>\textup{Span}\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}</m> which
        is the same as <m>\textup{Span}\left\{\begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 3 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} \right\}</m>.</p>
      <p>The <m>\pi</m>-eigenspace of <m>A</m> is
        <m>\textup{Span}\left\{\begin{pmatrix}4 \\ 2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>, which is the same as
        <m>\textup{Span}\left\{\begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 1 \\ \frac{1}{2}\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>.</p>
      <p>Therefore, by the Diagonalization Theorem, <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are two ways
        of writing the same matrix.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>More practice with diagonalization.</p>
      <p>Consider the matrix
      <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}-1\amp 5\amp 10 \\ 0\amp 4\amp 10 \\0 \amp 0 \amp 5 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Find the eigenvalues of <m>A</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>The matrix is upper-triangular, so its eigenvalues are
            its diagonal entries: <m>\lambda = -1</m>, <m>\lambda = 4</m>, and <m>\lambda = 5</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>For each eigenvalue of <m>A</m>, compute a basis for the
          corresponding eigenspace.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>The <m>(-1)</m>-eigenspace is <m>\Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>.</p>
        <p>The <m>4</m>-eigenspace is <m>\Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>.</p>
        <p>The <m>5</m>-eigenspace is <m>\Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}10 \\ 10 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Decide whether <m>A</m> is diagonalizable. If it is
          diagonalizable, find an invertible <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix <m>P</m> and a
          diagonal matrix <m>D</m> such that <m>A = PDP^{-1}</m>. If <m>A</m> is not
          diagonalizable, explain why.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>A</m> is diagonalizable: <m>A = PDP^{-1}</m> where the diagonal entries
              of <m>D</m> are the eigenvalues and the columns of <m>P</m> are eigenvectors written
              in the corresponding order.  Many answers are possible, for example:
          <md><mrow>P = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 1 \amp 10 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 10 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}, \qquad D = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 4 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 5\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <solution>
      <p>For this problem, we give the answers and leave the
        computations to the reader.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>More practice with diagonalization.</p>
      <p>Let <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</m>.  Is <m>A</m> diagonalizable?  If so, find a
      basis of <m>{\bf R}^3</m> consisting of eigenvectors of <m>A</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>For this problem, we give the answers and leave the computations to the
        reader.</p>
      <p>Solving <m>\det(A-\lambda I) = 0</m> gives <m>\lambda = 1</m> and <m>\lambda = 2</m>.</p>
      <p>The <m>1</m>-eigenspace is <m>\Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}0 \\ -1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>.</p>
      <p>The <m>2</m>-eigenspace is
        <m>\Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m>.</p>
      <p>
        The set <m>\left\{\begin{pmatrix}0 \\ -1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} \right\}</m> is a basis
          for <m>{\bf R}^{3}</m> consisting of eigenvectors for <m>A</m>, so <m>A</m> is
          diagonalizable.  In fact, <m>A = PDP^{-1}</m> where the diagonal entries of <m>D</m> are
          the eigenvalues and the columns of <m>P</m> are eigenvectors written in the
          corresponding order.  Many answers are possible, for example:
        <md><mrow>P = \begin{pmatrix}0 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\ -1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}, \qquad D = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</exercises>
</section>
